If HTTP is stateless, how does a website remember you after login?

HTTP is stateless, so websites remember logged-in users using cookies, sessions, or tokens like JWT. In distributed systems such as Kubernetes, sessions are typically stored in Redis or replaced with stateless JWT authentication to support scaling.


It is stateless, but uses some extra mechanisms on the top like, sessions, cookies using which user can be logged in even tough user gives new requests to the server


HTTP is stateless, but websites maintain user state using cookies, sessions, or authentication tokens that are sent with each request to identify the user.


